{
  "primary_meaning": "Christ as the firstfruits of the resurrection (linked to the wave-sheaf offering)",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Leviticus 23:9-14 (barley wave sheaf / firstfruits offering at Passover season)",
    "1 Corinthians 15:20-23 (Christ the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep)",
    "Agricultural calendar of Israel in which barley ripens first, coinciding with Unleavened Bread / Firstfruits"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "food of the poor, humility, or lowly provision",
      "basis": [
        "John 6:9 (five barley loaves)",
        "2 Kings 4:42 (barley loaves multiplied by Elisha)",
        "Ruth 1–2 (gleaning during barley harvest)",
        "Ezekiel 4:9 (barley as siege bread)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "humble instrument of unexpected victory",
      "basis": [
        "Judges 7:13 (barley cake tumbling into the Midianite camp in Gideon’s dream)"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "bitterness or jealousy (grain offering without oil or frankincense)",
      "basis": [
        "Numbers 5:15 (barley flour in the ordeal of jealousy)"
      ],
      "prominence": "rare"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": false,
  "confidence": "medium",
  "notes": "Barley is not among the most fixed biblical symbols; the firstfruits/resurrection association is the strongest recurring typology in Christian teaching on the feasts, while socio-economic readings (poverty/humility) are frequent in narrative commentaries. No single meaning dominates every tradition."
}
